The performance problem in `Review.cpp` is caused by **avoidable reallocations from repeated appends** within the loop. Since `std::string out` starts empty, each `+=` operation may trigger a new memory allocation and a copy of the existing content as the string grows.

The idiomatic fix is to **reserve** the total required memory upfront when the total size is known, which ensures only a single allocation occurs.

```cpp
std::string join(const std::vector<std::string>& parts) {
  size_t total_size = 0;
  for (const auto& p : parts) total_size += p.size();

  std::string out;
  out.reserve(total_size); // Idiomatic fix
  for (const auto& p : parts) out += p;
  return out;
}
```

